==Chinese==

===Glyph origin===
{{Han etyl}}
{{liushu|ic}} – two pieces of meat ({{zh-l|*肉}}). In the bronze script, {{zh-l|*肉}} was corrupted into {{zh-l|*夕}} due to visual similarity, making {{zh-l|*多}} into a {{zh-etym-double|夕|nocap=y}}. The form with {{zh-l|*夕}} was inherited in later scripts.

{{zh-ref|Chi (2010)}} suggests that meat is scarce in ancient times, so two pieces of meat is a lot, citing a passage from ''{{w|Mencius (book)|Mencius}}'':
: {{zh-x|七十 者 可以 食 肉 矣。|Persons of seventy years may eat meat.|collapsed=y}}

Chang Ping-chuan suggests that it is the duplicative nature of the character that gives the meaning of "many", just like in {{zh-l|*林|forest}}, from {{zh-l|*木|tree; wood}}.

===Etymology 1===
{{zh-forms|alt=𡖈,𢑑,夛,dur-Cantonese nonstandard:doe1}}

Etymology unclear. {{zh-ref|Schuessler (2007)}} suggests that it is in the same word family as {{och-l|諸|many; all}} and {{och-l|庶|many}}.

{{zh-ref|Hill (2014, 2019)}} compares it to {{cog|bo|ཆེ||big}}, {{m|bo|'''མཐེ'''་བོ||thumb}}, {{cog|my|တယ်||very}}. STEDT derives the latter two from {{cog|sit-pro|*ta-j||big}}, whose Chinese comparandum is {{och-l|大|id=1|large}} instead; {{zh-ref|Baxter (1992)}} has also compared it to this etymon.

{{zh-ref|Baxter and Sagart (1998)}} propose that there may be a prefix {{m|und||*t-}} in this word that gives a mass noun reading, which may nullify the connection to {{cog|sit-pro|*ta-j||big}}. However, {{zh-ref|Baxter and Sagart (2014)}} may have withdrawn from this theory since they no longer indicate the ''*t'' as a prefix.

Also compare {{cog|tai-pro|*ʰlaːjᴬ||many, much}}, whence {{cog|th|หลาย||many}} and {{cog|za|lai||many}}, and {{cog|qfa-lic-pro|*hləːy||many}} ({{zh-ref|Gong Qunhu, 2002}}; {{zh-ref|Schuessler, 2007}}; {{zh-ref|Baxter and Sagart, 2014}}). {{zh-ref|Schuessler (2007)}} considers the Chinese word to be from {{cog|qfa-tak}}, but others suggest that the direction of borrowing may have been the other way ({{zh-ref|Li, 1977}}; {{zh-ref|Baxter and Sagart, 1998}}; {{zh-ref|Norquest, 2007}}).

