===Etymology 1===
{{etystub|zh}}
; Zhou dynastic surname
: Its [[eponym]] was traditionally held to the river {{zh-l|姬|tr=Jī}}, which has been identified with the river {{zh-l|汾|tr=Fén}} in {{w|Shanxi}}. 

: However, {{zh-ref|Pulleyblank (2000)}} points out that "there seems to be no record of a Ji River outside the myth" of the {{w|Yellow Emperor}} Instead, he suggests an etymological connection between "the two intermarrying moieties, a ruling clan and its collateral, wife-supplying counterpart": {{och-l|姬|tr=*(C)ɰə̀ɰ}} and {{zh-l|姜|tr=*(C)ɰàŋ}}. He also points to other word-pairs, including {{ltc-l|似|to resemble}} < *-''əɰʔ'' and {{ltc-l|像|to imitate, image}} < *''-aŋʔ'', which display the phonetic correspondences: *''-ɰ'' vs. *''-ŋ'' as well *''-ə-'' vs. *''-a-'' (ibid.)

; lady {{n-g|(ancient laudatory title for women)}}
: According to {{w|Yan Shigu}} (apud {{w|Kangxi Dictionary}}):
{{zh-x|姬 本 ^周 姓，其 女 貴 於 列國 之 女，所以 婦人 美-號 皆 稱 姬。|'''''Ji'' ("姬")''' was originally the surname of the {{w|Zhou dynasty|Zhou}}; their princesses were more honorable than princesses of other states; therefore women with laudatory appellations are all called '''''Ji'' ("姬")'''.|CL|ref=Kangxi zidian|collapsed=yes}}

; "gay"
: Homophonous with {{zh-l|基}} or {{cog|en|gay}} (in Cantonese) and already carrying feminine connotations. 

